items.a:
The classic digit-sum with while loop fails for input 0 because the condition (n != 0) is immediately false. Do-while handles 0 correctly because the body always runs at least once:

int number = 0;  // Works correctly for 0!
int sum = 0;
int temp = Math.abs(number);  // Handle negative numbers
do {
    sum += temp % 10;  // 0 % 10 = 0, so sum = 0 for input 0
    temp /= 10;
} while (temp != 0);
System.out.println("Sum of digits: " + sum); // 0

With a while loop: while(temp != 0) — the body would never execute for temp=0, leaving sum=0 coincidentally correct but structurally wrong. Do-while is the semantically correct choice for digit operations.

