items.q:
6. Identify and fix all bugs in this code:
int i = 1
do {
    System.out.println(i);
} while (i <= 5)

items.a:
Two bugs:
Bug 1: Missing semicolon after int i = 1 — should be int i = 1; (compile error).
Bug 2: Missing semicolon after while(i <= 5) — should be while(i <= 5); (compile error).
Bug 3 (logic): i is never updated inside the body — infinite loop! Body prints 1 forever.

Fully fixed code:
int i = 1;  // ✅ semicolon added
do {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;    // ✅ update added
} while (i <= 5);  // ✅ semicolon added
// Output: 1 2 3 4 5

int row = 1;
do {
    int col = 1;
    do {
        System.out.print(row + " ");  // Print the row number, not col
        col++;
    } while (col <= row);           // Print 'row' times
    System.out.println();           // New line after each row
    row++;
} while (row <= 5);
// Key: the value printed is 'row' (not 'col').
// Inner loop runs 'row' times per outer iteration.
// col is declared INSIDE the outer loop so it resets to 1 each time.

